<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Prompts for Hint Generation</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following prompts to generate hints. First, we generate an explanation style of Chain-of-Thought by conditioning on the ground truth answer:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A4.p2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A4.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Question: <span id="A4.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{question}</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A4.p2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A4.p2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">I was told the answer is <span id="A4.p2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{gold_answer}</span> but I don’t know why.
Please explain why the answer is <span id="A4.p2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{gold_answer}</span> step by step.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we use the concatenation of the question with the generated explanation, and ask the model to generate an abstract hint using the following prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A4.p4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A4.p4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;system_prompt&gt;</span>
You are a tutor. You are given a set of question, correct answer and solution. Your job is to provide a hint for the problem. The hint should help the student learn the core concept (e.g. formula, lemma, or necessary knowledge) needed to solve this problem. The hint should be concise, to the point, but high level. Do not include any detailed steps or calculations or the final answer.
<span id="A4.p4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/system_prompt&gt;</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A4.p4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A4.p4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Question: <span id="A4.p4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{question}</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A4.p4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A4.p4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Answer: <span id="A4.p4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{gold_answer}</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A4.p4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A4.p4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Solution: <span id="A4.p4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{explanation}</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A4.p4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A4.p4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, please provide a hint for this problem to help the student learn the core concept.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Qualitative Examples</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Examples of Different Types of Hints</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide examples of the four types of hints we explored in this work. All the examples below answer the same question (shown below) and are self-generated with Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> During a school football match, there are 3 types of balls used, each with a different number of colors. Type A balls have yellow, brown, and blue variations; Type B balls have green, orange, and pink; and Type C balls have black, red, and white variations. The number of colored balls varies according to the number of players in each team. Team 1 has 72 members, and each player receives 4 Type A yellow balls, 3 Type A brown balls, and 3 Type A blue balls. Team 2 has 64 members, and each player receives 5 Type B green balls, 4 Type B orange balls, and 2 Type B pink balls. Team 3 has 53 members, and each player receives 3 Type C black balls, 2 Type C red balls, and 6 Type C white balls. Calculate what percentage of all the balls distributed for the match are Type A yellow balls.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.p2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer:</span> 14.35%</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To find the percentage of a specific type of ball among all distributed balls, first calculate the total number of that specific ball and then divide it by the total number of all balls distributed—then multiply by 100. Focus on identifying which team(s) contribute to the specific ball type and sum all balls across teams.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.p4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Step 1: We are to find the percentage of all the balls distributed that are Type A yellow balls. So, we need: (Total number of Type A yellow balls) / (Total number of all balls distributed) <math id="A5.SS1.p4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 100%</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.p4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Step 2: Calculate total number of Type A yellow balls. Team 1 has 72 members, and each player gets 4 Type A yellow balls. So Type A yellow balls = 72 <math id="A5.SS1.p4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 4 = 288.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Understand what is being asked.</span>
We are to calculate the percentage of all the balls distributed that are Type A yellow balls.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Calculate the number of Type A yellow balls.</span>
Team 1 has 72 members. Each player gets 4 Type A yellow balls.
Thus, <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Type A yellow balls}=72\times 4=288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Type A yellow balls</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Type A yellow balls}=72\times 4=288</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Calculate total number of balls distributed.</span>
We need to compute the total across all teams and all ball types.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Team 1 (Type A balls):</span> <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72\times(4+3+3)=72\times 10=720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>720</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72\times(4+3+3)=72\times 10=720</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Team 2 (Type B balls):</span> <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times(5+4+2)=64\times 11=704" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>704</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times(5+4+2)=64\times 11=704</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Team 3 (Type C balls):</span> <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53\times(3+2+6)=53\times 11=583" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>53</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>53</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>583</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53\times(3+2+6)=53\times 11=583</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Add up total balls across all teams.</span>
<math id="A5.SS1.p5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Total balls}=720+704+583=2007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Total balls</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>720</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>704</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>583</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2007</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Total balls}=720+704+583=2007</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5: Calculate percentage of Type A yellow balls.</span>
We already have <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Type A yellow balls}=288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Type A yellow balls</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Type A yellow balls}=288</annotation></semantics></math>.
So, <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Percentage}=\left(\frac{288}{2007}\right)\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Percentage</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>288</mn><mn>2007</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Percentage}=\left(\frac{288}{2007}\right)\times 100</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Simplify: <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{288}{2007}=\tfrac{96}{669}=\tfrac{32}{223}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>288</mn><mn>2007</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>96</mn><mn>669</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>32</mn><mn>223</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{288}{2007}=\tfrac{96}{669}=\tfrac{32}{223}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{32}{223}\approx 0.1435" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>32</mn><mn>223</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.1435</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{32}{223}\approx 0.1435</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1435\times 100=14.35\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.1435</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>14.35</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1435\times 100=14.35\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final Answer:</span> <math id="A5.SS1.p5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{14.35\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mn>14.35</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{14.35\%}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why is this correct?</span></span>
<span id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Only Team 1 gets Type A yellow balls.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total Type A yellow balls <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=72\times 4=288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=72\times 4=288</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total balls distributed <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=720+704+583=2007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>720</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>704</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>583</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2007</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=720+704+583=2007</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="A5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(288/2007)\times 100\approx 14.35\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>288</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2007</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>14.35</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(288/2007)\times 100\approx 14.35\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.p5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <span id="A5.SS1.p5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14.35% of all distributed balls are Type A yellow balls.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.p6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.p6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I was told the answer is <math id="A5.SS1.p6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><mrow><mn>14.35</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></math>. Do not blindly accept it. Take it as a reference and provide your own step-by-step reasoning.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.1.1 </span>Examples of Self- and GPT-o4-mini-generated Abstract Cues</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> What is the maximum amount of heat that can be supplied to the system during the electrolysis of water without violating the second law of thermodynamics? (A) 237 kJ (B) 286 kJ (C) 163 kJ (D) 49 kJ (Correct answer: D)</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hint (Self-generated): </span>
Think about the difference between the energy required to break water (enthalpy) and the useful energy available (Gibbs free energy). This difference represents the minimum amount of energy that must be lost as heat due to entropy—this is a direct consequence of the second law of thermodynamics. What is this difference for water electrolysis?</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hint (GPT-o4-mini-generated):</span>
Use the relation <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>G = <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>H – T<math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>S to find the entropy change, then recall that under a reversible (second‐law‐limit) process the maximum heat input is <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> = T<math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>S.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> When two fair dice are thrown, the numbers obtained are respectively <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Determine the probability that the line <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bx+ay=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bx+ay=1</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the circle <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}+y^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}+y^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and that the length of the intercepted chord does not exceed <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{4\sqrt{2}}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{4\sqrt{2}}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (Correct answer: <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{9}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hint (Self-generated): </span>
Focus on the geometric condition for a line to cut a chord of a given length in a unit circle. Recall that the length of the chord depends on the distance from the center of the circle to the line — use this relationship to set up an inequality involving <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then translate the condition into a constraint on <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}+b^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}+b^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hint (GPT-o4-mini-generated):</span>
Use the fact that for the circle <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}+y^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}+y^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the line <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bx+ay=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bx+ay=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the distance from the origin to the line is <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the chord‐length is <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2\sqrt{1-d^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2\sqrt{1-d^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L\leq\frac{4\sqrt{2}}{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L\leq\frac{4\sqrt{2}}{3})</annotation></semantics></math> to get a condition on <math id="A5.SS1.SSS1.p2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}+b^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}+b^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then count the integer pairs.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
